# Grandma Ruth 29 · Christmas tips: Christmas tree mistakes (Google Trends 3/11: tree-topper, when to put up decorations rising)
# Grandma Ruth: born 1944 in Mansfield, Ohio; mother Dorothy, father Walter, brother Danny, Grandma Pearl.

sc("""Sweetheart, I have decorated eighty-one Christmas trees in my life. I counted. The first one I remember, I was three years old, and my father lifted me up to put the star on top. And in all those years, I've learned that a beautiful tree has almost nothing to do with how much you spend. It has to do with seven little mistakes that almost everybody makes.""",
   "father lifting child star Christmas tree", "EIGHTY-ONE TREES")
sc("""Every year people send me pictures of their trees. Grandma, why does mine look thin? Why does it look cheap? Why does it look like a lamp post with things hanging off it? So tonight I'm giving you the seven Christmas tree mistakes, and for every one, the old trick that fixes it. Do these before you hang a single ornament this year. And stay with me, because the last one isn't about looks at all. It's about keeping your family safe.""",
   "sparse thin Christmas tree living room", "LIKE A LAMP POST")

ch("When to put it up")
sc("""First, the question everybody asks me. When should you put up the tree? When I was a girl in Mansfield, nobody had a tree before the middle of December. Real trees dried out in a warm house, and they were a fire danger with the old hot bulbs. Most families put the tree up a week or two before Christmas, and some, like Grandma Pearl, waited until Christmas Eve.""",
   "1950s family carrying Christmas tree home", "WHEN TO PUT IT UP")
sc("""Today, with artificial trees and cool little lights, lots of families put the tree up the weekend after Thanksgiving, and there's nothing wrong with that. My rule is simple. Artificial tree, the day after Thanksgiving if you want. A real tree, no earlier than the first weekend of December, so it's still fresh and green on Christmas morning, and not dropping needles all over the presents.""",
   "family decorating tree after Thanksgiving", "MY RULE")

ch("1. Not fluffing the branches")
sc("""Mistake number one, and it's the biggest one with artificial trees. Not fluffing the branches. When the tree comes out of the box, the branches are flat and squished from eleven months in the attic. People bend them down and start hanging ornaments, and the tree looks thin with big holes you can see right through.""",
   "artificial tree flat branches box", "SQUISHED FROM THE ATTIC")
sc("""The fix takes about an hour, and it's worth every minute. Start at the bottom, and work on one branch at a time. Pull every little tip out, some up, some down, some to the sides, like fingers on a spread hand. Work from the inside of the branch near the trunk out to the tip. Put on gloves. Those plastic needles are sharper than they look.""",
   "fluffing artificial tree branches hands", "LIKE FINGERS ON A HAND")

ch("2. Too few lights")
sc("""Mistake number two. Too few lights. One string of a hundred lights on a seven-foot tree looks like a few fireflies lost in the dark. The old decorators' rule is about one hundred lights for every foot of tree. So a seven-foot tree wants about seven hundred lights. That sounds like a lot, honey. Hang them, and you'll see. In a minute I'll show you how to hang them so the tree glows from the inside.""",
   "Christmas tree glowing many lights", "A HUNDRED LIGHTS PER FOOT")

sc("""And mix your bulbs right, honey. Don't put warm white lights and cold blue-white lights on the same tree. The cold ones look like a hospital hallway next to the warm ones. Pick one color of white and stick with it. For the old-fashioned look I love, warm white, the color of candlelight. If you love the colored lights like I had as a girl, go all colored, and go big.""",
   "warm white Christmas lights close", "ONE COLOR OF WHITE")

ch("3. Lights only on the outside")
sc("""Mistake number three. Wrapping the lights around the outside of the tree like a candy cane stripe. That's how most people do it, and it's why the tree looks flat. The light sits on the surface, and the inside of the tree is a dark hole. A tree that glows from the inside looks deep and full and rich.""",
   "lights wrapped around outside tree", "A DARK HOLE INSIDE")
sc("""The fix. Work branch by branch. Start at the trunk, run the string out along the top of a branch to the tip, then back to the trunk along the underside, and on to the next branch. In and out, in and out. It takes longer, and it's the single biggest difference between a tree that looks cheap and a tree that looks like a department store window.""",
   "lights along tree branch inside out", "IN AND OUT")
sc("""And always check the lights before they go on the tree. Plug in every string on the floor first. There's nothing worse than finishing the whole tree and finding one string dead in the middle. My father had a ritual. He laid all the strings out across the living room rug on the first Saturday, plugged them in one by one, and replaced every burned-out bulb before a single one went on the tree. If this is helping you, honey, tap the like button for me. It helps Grandma more than you know.""",
   "Christmas lights laid out on floor", "CHECK THEM ON THE FLOOR")

ch("4. Wrong order")
sc("""Mistake number four. Decorating in the wrong order. People put on ornaments, then remember the garland, and they have to wrap it around and knock everything off. The order is always the same, and it hasn't changed since my mother's day. Lights first. Then garland or ribbon. Then the big ornaments, then the small ones. And the topper goes on before the ornaments, not after.""",
   "decorating Christmas tree garland", "ALWAYS THE SAME ORDER")
sc("""Why the topper early? Because you need to reach the very top, and you'll be leaning on the step ladder against the tree. If the ornaments are already on, you'll knock half of them off. My father learned that the hard way in nineteen fifty-three, when the ladder slipped and eleven glass balls hit the floor. My mother didn't speak to him until supper.""",
   "broken glass ornaments floor", "ELEVEN GLASS BALLS")

ch("5. Ornaments all on the outside")
sc("""Mistake number five. Every ornament hung on the very tips of the branches, all in a single layer. It looks flat, and the branches droop. Here's the trick the window decorators use. Hang some of your big, plain, cheap ornaments deep inside the tree, close to the trunk. They catch the light from inside and fill the holes. Then you'll see why the special ones go last.""",
   "ornaments deep inside tree branches", "HANG SOME DEEP INSIDE")
sc("""Then the medium ones in the middle, and your special ornaments, the pretty glass ones and the ones with memories, out near the tips where everybody can see them. Big ones on the bottom, small ones on top, so the tree looks balanced. And hang them in clusters of three of the same kind, not spaced evenly like polka dots.""",
   "special ornaments tree branches close", "CLUSTERS OF THREE")

sc("""And here's something for the families with little ones and pets. Keep the glass and the heirloom ornaments on the top half of the tree, and put the unbreakable ones on the bottom two feet. Wooden ones, felt ones, the ones the children made at school. The cat will bat at them and the baby will pull them off, and nothing will break, and nobody will cry.""",
   "child and cat near Christmas tree", "NOBODY WILL CRY")

ch("6. The wrong topper")
sc("""Mistake number six. A tree topper that's too small, or too heavy. A tiny star on a big tree looks like a forgotten afterthought. And a big heavy angel makes the top bend over like it's tired. The topper should be about one tenth the height of the tree, and light enough that the top stands straight.""",
   "Christmas tree topper star angel", "ONE TENTH THE HEIGHT")
sc("""If the top branch won't hold your topper up, here's my mother's trick. Tie a pencil or a wooden chopstick to the top of the trunk with a piece of wire or florist tape, so it sticks up straight, and slide the topper over that. It stands straight all season. And if you want a fuller look, tuck a few sprays of ribbon or picks behind the star, fanning out.""",
   "tying stick to tree top", "A PENCIL AND WIRE")

ch("7. Forgetting the water")
sc("""And mistake number seven, for real trees, and this one is about safety. Forgetting the water. A fresh-cut tree can drink a gallon of water the first day. If the stand runs dry even once, the cut end seals over with sap, and the tree can't drink anymore. It dries out, the needles drop, and a dry tree burns fast. I'll show you how to keep that from happening.""",
   "Christmas tree stand water", "A GALLON THE FIRST DAY")
sc("""So when you buy the tree, ask them to cut half an inch off the bottom of the trunk, and put it in water within a few hours. Check the stand every single day, morning and night. Keep the tree away from the fireplace, the heater vents and the radiator. And always turn off the lights when you go to bed or leave the house. My father checked the water every morning before his coffee, without fail.""",
   "checking tree stand water morning", "EVERY MORNING")

ch("The finishing touch")
sc("""And one last thing, the touch that makes the whole room feel like Christmas. A tree skirt, or an old quilt, or a white sheet bunched around the bottom, to cover the stand. A bare metal stand at the bottom of a beautiful tree is like a beautiful dress with muddy shoes. My mother used a white sheet with cotton batting for snow, and a little wooden train set running in a circle.""",
   "tree skirt presents under tree", "MUDDY SHOES")
sc("""Then turn off every other light in the room, sit down on the couch, and look at it from across the room. Squint your eyes. Any dark spots, you'll see right away. Move a few lights, move a few ornaments. That's what my mother did, every year, with her cup of coffee. Then she'd say, there, now it's Christmas.""",
   "glowing Christmas tree dark living room", "NOW IT'S CHRISTMAS")

ch("Grandma's tree")
sc("""So, one more time. Fluff every branch. A hundred lights for every foot. Wrap them in and out of each branch, not around the outside. Lights, garland, topper, then ornaments. Plain ones deep inside, special ones on the tips, in threes. A topper one tenth the height. And for a real tree, water every single day. That's how you get a tree that looks like a picture.""",
   "beautiful decorated Christmas tree", "LIKE A PICTURE")
sc("""Tell me in the comments, sweetheart. What's on top of your tree? A star, an angel, a bow? And who in your family gets to put it up there? In my house it's always the youngest child who can walk. And if you want another one tonight, the whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips is right there on your screen, and I'll see you in the next video. Good night, honey. Merry Christmas.""",
   "child placing star top of tree", "WHAT'S ON YOUR TREE?")

for s in S:
    s["cuts"], s["moves"] = 4, True

save("grandma29", "7 Christmas Tree Mistakes EVERYONE Makes (Why Your Tree Looks CHEAP)",
     ["Grandma Ruth is a narrated character built from real memories and records of the era.",
      "Common decorator's guideline of about 100 lights per foot of tree height.",
      "Christmas tree safety: fresh cut, water daily, keep away from heat sources (U.S. Fire Administration and National Christmas Tree Association guidance)."],
     "christmas with tips", minutes=(10, 14.5), voice="en_US-lessac-high")
